    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Understanding Specificity and Overrides (Practically)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "CSS doesn't apply styles randomly. It follows rules."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The most important one for debugging: More specific selectors win."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<main class=\"container\">\n2  <p>This paragraph is outside <code>.article</code>, so it becomes black.</p>\n3\n4  <article class=\"article\">\n5    <h2>Inside Article</h2>\n6    <p>This paragraph is inside <code>.article</code>, so it becomes gray.</p>\n7    <p>This one is also gray due to the more specific selector.</p>\n8  </article>\n9</main>"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "css",
      "content": "1p {\n2  color: black;\n3}\n4\n5.article p {\n6  color: gray;\n7}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Paragraphs inside .article will be gray."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Developer Insight"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "When a style \"doesn't work\", it usually means:"
    },
    {
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "items": [
        "Another rule is winning",
        "Or your selector isn't specific enough"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
